================================================================================
The contract dataset
================================================================================

src/main/resources/contract-dataset.json is generated by tools/build-contract-dataset.py
from the vanilla client assets that misode/mcmeta (https://github.com/misode/mcmeta)
publishes under a per-version `<id>-assets` tag. That repository is a mirror of files
Mojang ships and carries no licence of its own; it was used as a read-only reference.

The dataset contains no Mojang asset. It records structural facts about them: which
file names exist under shaders/core, shaders/include, shaders/post and post_effect;
the name, member names, member types and member order of each `layout(std140)
uniform` block; the `#version` number; which include directive is used; whether
include files carry a `#version` line; the shape a post effect definition takes; and
the vertex shader post passes reference. Those facts are what a pack author has to
match, and they are stated so that the tool works offline.

The fixture pack under src/test/resources/oldpack was written for this project in the
shape that plugins ship shaders in. It is not a copy of any vanilla or third-party file.

================================================================================
Where the facts come from
================================================================================

  That shaders/core/*.json program definitions and shaders/post/*.json program
  configs stopped being read at 1.21.5 was established by listing the directory
  at every tag from 1.17 to 26.3-pre-1: 56 JSON files at 1.21.4, zero from 1.21.5.

  The uniform blocks, their members and their order per version were extracted
  from shaders/include/*.glsl and from the core programs that declare blocks of
  their own (clouds.vsh, lightmap.fsh, animate_sprite.vsh, terrain.vsh, gui.vsh).

  That `LineWidth` leaves DynamicTransforms at 1.21.11 — not 1.21.9 — was read from
  include/dynamictransforms.glsl at 1.21.9, 1.21.10 and 1.21.11. A source comment in
  Auxilor/EcoItems places the change at 1.21.9; the vanilla files disagree.

  The 26.3 reorder of DynamicTransforms, the change from #moj_import to #include,
  the removal of #version from include files, the layout(location) requirement and
  the gl_VertexID to gl_VertexIndex rename were read by comparing the same files at
  26.2 and 26.3-pre-1. The vanilla comment in core/gui.vsh explaining why startup
  shaders inline their blocks instead of importing them is quoted nowhere here but
  is the reason the checker treats an inlined block the same as an included one.

  The post effect definition shapes were read from post_effect/*.json at 1.21.4
  (program references), 1.21.5 (list-form uniforms) and 1.21.6 onward (map-form
  uniforms keyed by block name), and the vertex shader post passes reference from
  the same files at 1.21.8 and 1.21.9.

  The /posteffect command and the minecraft:end_of_frame hook were confirmed in
  the command tree misode/mcmeta publishes under its summary branch — absent at
  26.2 and the first two 26.3 snapshots, present from 26.3-snapshot-3 — and in the
  26.3-snapshot-3 changelog on minecraft.wiki.

  The resource pack format for each version comes from misode/mcmeta's version
  summary, which mirrors Mojang's version manifest.

================================================================================
Prior art
================================================================================

None of these projects' code is used. Reading them shaped what this tool does.

  JNNGL/vanilla-shaders         MIT           https://github.com/JNNGL/vanilla-shaders
                                The reference collection of what a pack could do
                                with core shaders through 1.21.4. Every one of its
                                packs uses the program JSON that 1.21.5 removed,
                                which is the clearest illustration of the problem.

  toxicity188/BetterHud         MIT           https://github.com/toxicity188/BetterHud
                                Ships generated shaders through pack overlays keyed
                                to pack format ranges. The right shape, and the one
                                the range check is meant to feed. Its own overlay
                                table was cross-checked against the dataset and
                                found correct.

  Auxilor/EcoItems              GPL-3.0       https://github.com/Auxilor/EcoItems
                                Generates a gui.vsh with the uniform blocks inlined.
                                Read as evidence of what plugins emit; its comment
                                about when LineWidth left is the example in the
                                README of a fact that drifted.

  ComunidadAylas/PackSquash     AGPL-3.0      https://github.com/ComunidadAylas/PackSquash
                                A real GLSL parser and pack optimiser. Run it after
                                this, as a separate program.

  Godlander/objmc               MIT           https://github.com/Godlander/objmc
                                The one generator in the domain, and a pack that
                                still ships a core JSON at the time of writing.

  seailz/shader-devtools        MIT           https://github.com/seailz/shader-devtools
                                A client mod that lists the engine's render
                                pipelines live, which is the only place the
                                per-pipeline defines can be seen. This tool does
                                not attempt to reproduce that from data.

  McTsts/Minecraft-Shaders-Wiki (no licence)  https://github.com/McTsts/Minecraft-Shaders-Wiki
                                The community's map from render type to visual,
                                last annotated for 1.21.1. Used to understand the
                                domain; nothing from it is reproduced.

  CaffeineMC/sodium             PolyForm Shield 1.0.0
                                Read only to establish which vanilla shaders it
                                replaces (the terrain programs) so the README does
                                not overstate the interaction.

